---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Content from "../Content.astro";
import SectionHead from "../SectionHead.astro";
import Link from "../Link.astro";
import EmbeddingPipeline from "./software/EmbeddingPipeline.astro";
import MorphospacePlot from "./biocosmos/MorphospacePlot.tsx";
import PlannerPlot from "./biocosmos/PlannerPlot.tsx";
import MimicryPlot from "./biocosmos/MimicryPlot.tsx";
import mimicry from "../../data/biocosmos/mimicry.json";
import stats from "../../data/biocosmos/morphospace-stats.json";
import lepiverseHome from "../../assets/img/biocosmos/lepiverse-home.jpg";
import lepiverseSpecies from "../../assets/img/biocosmos/lepiverse-species.jpg";
import lepiverseSimilar from "../../assets/img/biocosmos/lepiverse-similar-species.jpg";

const morphoStats = [
  { value: stats.species.toLocaleString(), label: "species in the morphospace" },
  { value: stats.bothSides.toLocaleString(), label: "seen from both wing sides" },
  { value: `${stats.generaSignificant}/${stats.genera}`, label: "genera with integrated dorsal and ventral patterns (Mantel p < 0.05)" },
  { value: `r = ${stats.scopeMantelR}`, label: "dorso-ventral integration across all species" },
];

// From the lepiverse.com collection summary (October 2026).
const collection = [
  { value: "619,739", label: "specimen images" },
  { value: "9,342", label: "species" },
  { value: "196", label: "countries" },
  { value: "8", label: "validated families" },
];

const random = mimicry.permutations.filter((p) => p.null === "Random");
const mimicryStats = [
  { value: `${mimicry.pairs.filter((p) => p.recovered).length}/${mimicry.counts.testedPairs}`, label: "pairs mutually in the top 10 of 8,409 species on at least one wing side" },
  { value: random.map((p) => p.observed.toFixed(3)).join(" · "), label: "mean partner percentile, dorsal · ventral (chance = 0.50)" },
  { value: "p ≤ 0.0001", label: "against random and congener nulls (10,000 permutations)" },
];

const figureClasses =
  "rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 bg-white/70 p-3 shadow-sm dark:border-forest-100/10 dark:bg-forest-950/40 sm:p-5";
const shotClasses =
  "group overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 bg-forest-50 p-1.5 transition duration-500 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-lg dark:border-forest-100/10 dark:bg-forest-900/40";
---

<SectionHead text="AI-assisted phenomics: quantifying complex traits at collection scale" />
<Content>
  <p>
    Coloration is a complex phenotype shaped by shared ancestry, convergence,
    and environmental selection. Using image embeddings from digitized
    specimens, we measure phenotypic similarity within and among species and
    have found latitudinal variation in color similarity alongside species
    richness. Butterflies are our test case: I am testing these patterns in
    butterfly mimicry by integrating image-based similarity with genetic
    relationships and geographic co-occurrence.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2">
    I hope to extend this framework to Indo-Australian rodents, asking
    whether phenotypic divergence among isolated island and mountain
    populations parallels genomic divergence or reflects local environments,
    and, through collaborations, to frogs, reptiles, and birds.
  </p>
  <EmbeddingPipeline />
</Content>

<SectionHead text="The butterfly morphospace: upper and under wings tell different stories" />
<Content>
  <p>
    Each point is a species centroid in a principal-component space of image
    embeddings from BioCosmos. Switch between the dorsal (upper) and ventral
    (under) wing surfaces to watch species shift: skippers (Hesperiidae) and
    whites and sulphurs (Pieridae) separate clearly, while many lineages change
    position between sides. Hover a point for its name, or pick a family to
    highlight it.
  </p>
</Content>
<figure class={`not-prose my-6 ${figureClasses}`}>
  <MorphospacePlot client:visible />
</figure>
<dl class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 lg:grid-cols-4">
  {
    morphoStats.map((s) => (
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 px-3 py-3 dark:border-forest-100/10">
        <dt class="sr-only">{s.label}</dt>
        <dd class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-moss-700 dark:text-moss-300">{s.value}</dd>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-xs text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300">{s.label}</dd>
      </div>
    ))
  }
</dl>

<SectionHead text="BioCosmos: AI-enabled exploration of biological collections" />
<Content>
  <p>
    <Link text="BioCosmos" url="https://lepiverse.com" /> is a low-cost,
    AI-enabled web platform deployed through LepiVerse that brings together more
    than 600,000 digitized Lepidoptera specimen images. It combines taxonomic
    harmonization and geographic data validation with visual-similarity search,
    natural-language search, interactive morphospace plots, and enriched species
    pages, showing that image embeddings and open-weight large language models
    can make large collections explorable. Next, I plan to extend
    embedding-based trait exploration across more Lepidoptera and connect
    phenotypes with taxonomic, geographic, and genomic data.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2">
    This work is part of my postdoc in the AI Biodiversity Informatics Division
    at the Florida Museum of Natural History, with <Link
      text="Porto's lab"
      url="https://agporto.github.io/"
    />, University of Florida.
  </p>
</Content>

<dl class="mx-auto mt-6 grid max-w-4xl grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-center sm:grid-cols-4">
  {
    collection.map((s) => (
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 px-3 py-4 dark:border-forest-100/10">
        <dt class="sr-only">{s.label}</dt>
        <dd class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-moss-700 dark:text-moss-300">{s.value}</dd>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-xs text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300">{s.label}</dd>
      </div>
    ))
  }
</dl>

<div class="my-6 grid gap-3 md:grid-cols-2">
  <a href="https://lepiverse.com" class={shotClasses}>
    <Image src={lepiverseHome} alt="LepiVerse home page with semantic, text, and image search beside a grid of butterfly specimens" width={900} loading="lazy" class="rounded-xl" />
    <p class="flex justify-between px-2 pt-1.5 text-xs">
      <span class="font-semibold text-forest-900 dark:text-forest-100">Semantic, text, and image search</span>
      <span class="font-mono text-forest-600 dark:text-forest-400">lepiverse.com</span>
    </p>
  </a>
  <a href="https://lepiverse.com/species/morpho_aega" class={shotClasses}>
    <Image src={lepiverseSpecies} alt="LepiVerse species page for Morpho aega with specimen images, classification, and distribution map" width={900} loading="lazy" class="rounded-xl" />
    <p class="flex justify-between px-2 pt-1.5 text-xs">
      <span class="font-semibold text-forest-900 dark:text-forest-100">Enriched species page</span>
      <span class="font-mono text-forest-600 dark:text-forest-400"><i>Morpho aega</i></span>
    </p>
  </a>
</div>
<figure class={shotClasses}>
  <Image
    src={lepiverseSimilar}
    alt="Visually similar species to Morpho aega. Dorsal matches are blue Morpho species; ventral matches include brown Morpho, Zeuxidia, Tanaecia, and Vindula species."
    width={1400}
    loading="lazy"
    class="w-full rounded-xl"
  />
  <figcaption class="px-2 pt-2 text-xs text-forest-800 dark:text-forest-200">
    <span class="font-semibold">Visually similar species.</span> Nearest neighbours
    of <i>Morpho aega</i> in embedding space. Dorsal matches are all blue
    <i>Morpho</i>, but the cryptic brown undersides also match <i>Zeuxidia</i>,
    <i>Tanaecia</i>, and <i>Vindula</i>, from different genera: the same
    upper/under contrast the morphospace shows.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<SectionHead text="Does visual similarity recover butterfly mimicry?" />
<Content>
  <p>
    Mimicry is a direct test of whether image embeddings capture what
    predators see. I compiled published Müllerian and Batesian butterfly
    mimicry pairs and asked where each partner ranks when either species
    queries every other species in the collection by the similarity of its
    dorsal or ventral images. Partners rank far above chance, and above the
    congeners that resemble each other through shared ancestry alone. The
    misses are informative: in <i>Papilio polytes</i> and <i>P. glaucus</i>
    only some females mimic, and <i>Heliconius timareta</i> varies across its
    range, so a single species centroid dilutes the mimetic form. Switch wing
    sides to watch the ranks move, hover a dot for its rank, or click a pair
    for its notes and references.
  </p>
</Content>
<figure class={`not-prose my-6 ${figureClasses}`}>
  <MimicryPlot client:visible />
</figure>
<dl class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
  {
    mimicryStats.map((s) => (
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 px-3 py-3 dark:border-forest-100/10">
        <dt class="sr-only">{s.label}</dt>
        <dd class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-moss-700 dark:text-moss-300">{s.value}</dd>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-xs text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300">{s.label}</dd>
      </div>
    ))
  }
</dl>

<SectionHead text="Choosing an open-weight LLM for natural-language search" />
<Content>
  <p>
    I designed the search features in BioCosmos, where a language model turns a
    question such as “orange butterflies with black stripes from Peru” into
    structured search calls (color, country, trait, or similarity). To choose
    the best model for production, I benchmark open-weight models on the same
    set of queries, comparing accuracy, consistency, latency, and token cost.
  </p>
</Content>
<figure class={`not-prose my-6 ${figureClasses}`}>
  <PlannerPlot client:visible />
</figure>
